==English==

===Etymology===
Arabic ''rightly guided one''

===Proper noun===
{{wikipedia}}
'''Mahdi'''


All the believers in revealed Scriptures, Jews, Christians and Muslims hold the belief in an expected deliverer i.e., a Mahdi who will restore and reform all things. The word "Al Mahdi" means the guided one. It is passive form of Huda meaning to direct. As such the word “The Mahdi” stands for one who is divinely guided or one who is fit to direct others.

Al-Tirmizi, Abu-Dawood, Al-Bazzar, Ibn-e-Maja, Al-Hakim & Al-Tabarani are among the important compilations of traditions in which there are traditions numbering over 300 about the Mahdi. Those who are associated in one way or other with these traditions include Ali Ibn Abu-Talib, Abdullah Ibn Abbas, Abdullah Ibn Umer, Talha, Abdullah Ibn Masood, Abusayeed Khudri, Umme Habibah, Umme salma, Sawban and others, may Allah be pleased with them all.

Hazrath Mahdi e Maood Alaihis Salaam is the Promised Mehdi of Jaunpur(INDIA)

Some of the popular traditions are:

(I)   How can my folk he ruined when I am at its beginning; Jesus, the son of Mary is at its end and the Mahdi from my progeny is in the middle.

(II)  The world will not come to an end unless God sends a person from the progeny of my family whose name will be my name and whose father's name will be my father's name and who will fill the world so much with justice as it would be filled with atrocities.

(III) He who does not believe in the Mahdi is an unbeliever and he who does not believe in anti-Christ is a liar.

It is reported in Insaf Nama that Meeran Syed Mohammed Jaunpuri who proclaimed himself as Imam Mahdi in 901 (1496 AD) had, on one occasion, said that God sent him at a time when Islam had lost its pristine glory due to social customs, personal habits and innovations running contrary to the Shariah i.e. the law of Islam.